I have everything I need. `derrick-tools` has zero `derrick-*` dependencies (true leaf), and `serde`/`serde_json` are already present. Here is the contract.

---

# Rust contract: collapsing model providers onto the host-CLI layer

## 1. Dependency edge: `derrick-models → derrick-tools`

**No cycle.** `derrick-tools/Cargo.toml` has no `derrick-*` dependencies — it is a true leaf (confirmed: only `async-trait`, `serde`, `serde_json`, `thiserror`, `tokio`, `which`). `derrick-tools` does **not** depend on `derrick-config` either, so the catalogue must be config-agnostic (it keys on plain `&str` host names, never on `Host`/`ModelDef`). The four crates that depend on `derrick-models` (`derrick-assay`, `derrick-cli`, `derrick-flow`, `derrick-adopt`) all already depend on `derrick-tools` or are downstream of it, so the new edge adds nothing to the existing DAG ordering.

Add to `derrick-models/Cargo.toml`:
```toml
derrick-tools = { path = "../derrick-tools" }
```

**What `derrick-models` imports from `derrick-tools`** (the entire public surface it needs):
- `HostAdapter` (the trait — for the boxed adapter object)
- `HostRequest`, `HostResponse` (request/response structs)
- `HostError` (for error mapping)
- `CopilotToolPermission` (to set `copilot_tools` on the request)
- The host constructors `ClaudeHost`, `CodexHost`, `CopilotHost`, `OpencodeHost`, and the new `AiderHost` — via `derrick_tools::hosts::*` or the re-exports
- `catalogue` (new module): `HostCatalogue`, or the free functions `catalogue::builtin()` / `catalogue::normalize(...)`

**`reqwest` can be dropped.** Its only consumers are `anthropic.rs` (`reqwest::Client`, SSE) and the `reqwest` header/error handling there. `openai_cli.rs`, `opencode.rs`, `subprocess.rs`, `shell.rs` use `tokio::process`/`shell_words`, not `reqwest`. Once `anthropic.rs` is deleted, `reqwest` has no users in the crate. `shell-words` should be re-audited too — it is only used by `subprocess.rs::parse_argv` and `shell.rs`; if `shell.rs` keeps parsing a configured command string it stays, otherwise it also drops. (Flag: verify `shell.rs` before removing `shell-words`.)

---

## 2. `derrick-tools/src/catalogue.rs`

Const-table, not lazy. The data is fully static (`&'static str` host names, model id slices, a `Copy` enum). A `match` on `host` returning borrowed `&'static` slices needs no allocation, no `OnceLock`, no `lazy_static`. `normalize` is the only function that allocates (it returns an owned `String` because it may strip a prefix). Keep it `pub` and dependency-free.

```rust
//! Curated host/model catalogue (May 2026). Single source of truth for
//! default models, `derrick models check`, and per-host id normalisation.
//! Owned by derrick-tools so both the in-crate adapters and derrick-cli can
//! reach it without a dependency cycle.

/// How a host CLI expects a `--model` argument to be spelled.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq)]
pub enum ModelIdStyle {
    /// Bare model id, any leading `provider/` is stripped (claude, codex, copilot).
    BareId,
    /// Fully-qualified `provider/model`, passed through verbatim (opencode, aider).
    ProviderModel,
}

/// Curated models for one host.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
pub struct HostModels {
    /// Host name, matching `HostAdapter::name()` and the registry key.
    pub host: &'static str,
    /// Default model id, or `None` for hosts with no curated default (opencode/aider).
    pub default_model: Option<&'static str>,
    /// Curated current model ids for this host.
    pub known: &'static [&'static str],
    /// How `--model` must be spelled for this host.
    pub id_style: ModelIdStyle,
}

/// The full curated catalogue.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
pub struct HostCatalogue {
    entries: &'static [HostModels],
}

impl HostCatalogue {
    /// Returns the entry for a host name, if curated.
    pub fn host(&self, host: &str) -> Option<&'static HostModels> { /* linear scan over entries */ }

    /// All curated host entries, stable order (claude, codex, copilot, opencode, aider).
    pub fn hosts(&self) -> &'static [HostModels] { self.entries }

    /// Whether `model` is in the curated `known` list for `host`.
    /// `false` when the host is unknown OR the model is not listed.
    /// Note: callers treat a `false` as WARN-not-FAIL per the hybrid-validation rule.
    pub fn is_known(&self, host: &str, model: &str) -> bool { /* ... */ }

    /// Normalises a model id for the given host's `id_style`:
    /// - `BareId`  : strip a single leading `provider/` segment if present.
    /// - `ProviderModel`: return verbatim.
    /// Unknown host: return verbatim (defensive; adapter still owns the final argv).
    /// Never translates dot<->dash (copilot keeps its dotted ids).
    pub fn normalize(&self, host: &str, model: &str) -> String { /* ... */ }
}

/// The baked-in May-2026 catalogue.
pub const fn builtin() -> HostCatalogue {
    const ENTRIES: &[HostModels] = &[
        HostModels { host: "claude",   default_model: Some("claude-opus-4-8"),
            known: &["claude-opus-4-8", "claude-sonnet-4-6", "claude-haiku-4-5"],
            id_style: ModelIdStyle::BareId },
        HostModels { host: "codex",    default_model: Some("gpt-5.5"),
            known: &["gpt-5.5", "gpt-5.4", "gpt-5.4-mini", "gpt-5.2-codex"],
            id_style: ModelIdStyle::BareId },
        HostModels { host: "copilot",  default_model: Some("gpt-5.4"),
            known: &["gpt-5.4", "gpt-5.3-codex", "claude-sonnet-4.6", "claude-haiku-4.5", "gpt-5.4-mini"],
            id_style: ModelIdStyle::BareId },
        HostModels { host: "opencode", default_model: None, known: &[/* curated provider/model */],
            id_style: ModelIdStyle::ProviderModel },
        HostModels { host: "aider",    default_model: None, known: &[/* curated provider/model */],
            id_style: ModelIdStyle::ProviderModel },
    ];
    HostCatalogue { entries: ENTRIES }
}
```

`builtin()` is `const fn` so the table is a true compile-time constant. Convenience free functions `pub fn is_known(host, model) -> bool` and `pub fn normalize(host, model) -> String` can wrap `builtin().is_known(...)` for the adapters that just want `catalogue::normalize(self.name(), model)`. Decide whether to expose both the method form and the free-function form — recommend free functions for the adapter call sites (terser) plus the `HostCatalogue` struct for the CLI which wants to iterate `hosts()`.

`normalize` strips exactly **one** leading `provider/` for `BareId`. Implementation: `model.split_once('/').map(|(_, rest)| rest).unwrap_or(model)` only when `id_style == BareId`. This handles `anthropic/claude-...`, `openai/gpt-...`, and any `provider/` for copilot in one branch; `ProviderModel` returns `model.to_owned()`.

---

## 3. Host-delegated provider (`derrick-models/src/providers/host_delegated.rs`)

### Ownership: construct the adapter per provider name, hold it as `Arc<dyn HostAdapter>`

`HostRegistry` is **not** `Clone` and owns `Box<dyn HostAdapter>`, so the provider cannot hold a registry by value and cannot clone one out. Each host is independently constructible via `XHost::new()`. The cleanest ownership: **the constructor closure (captured provider name) builds the one adapter it needs and stores it as `Arc<dyn HostAdapter>` inside the model.** `HostAdapter::run(&self, ...)` is `&self`, so an `Arc` shared reference is sufficient; no `&mut` or ownership transfer is needed. The hosts are zero-config unit structs, so constructing one per `ModelDef` is free.

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::time::Duration;

use async_trait::async_trait;
use derrick_config::ModelDef;
use derrick_tools::{
    catalogue, CopilotToolPermission, HostAdapter, HostError, HostRequest, HostResponse,
    hosts::{AiderHost, ClaudeHost, CodexHost, CopilotHost, OpencodeHost},
};

use crate::{
    builtin_cost_hint, AuthStore, CompletionEvent, CompletionRequest, CompletionStream,
    CostHint, FinishReason, Model, ModelError,
};

struct HostDelegatedModel {
    /// Configured model alias / id (what `name()` reports, pre-normalisation).
    name: String,
    /// Host name; also the `provider()` value and the catalogue key.
    host: &'static str,
    /// The one adapter for this host. `Arc` because `run()` is `&self`
    /// and the model is shared behind `Box<dyn Model>` (Send + Sync).
    adapter: Arc<dyn HostAdapter>,
    cost_hint: Option<CostHint>,
    /// Forwarded env (e.g. GH_TOKEN, proxy vars) captured at build time.
    env: std::collections::HashMap<String, String>,
}

/// Builds a host-delegated model for a fixed host name. One of these closures
/// is registered per host in `ProviderRegistry::with_defaults`.
fn build_for_host(
    host: &'static str,
    adapter: Arc<dyn HostAdapter>,
    model_def: &ModelDef,
    auth: &AuthStore,
) -> Result<Box<dyn Model>, ModelError> {
    let name = model_def.model().to_owned();
    let cost_hint = builtin_cost_hint(&name);
    Ok(Box::new(HostDelegatedModel {
        name,
        host,
        adapter,
        cost_hint,
        env: auth.env_map(), // see §5
    }))
}
```

### `Model` impl — one-shot stream from a batch `run()`

`complete()` is **untouched**: the default method drains whatever `stream()` yields. The provider emits exactly two events (`Content` then `End`), so the default `complete()` reassembles the full text and the real token counts for free. This is what `derrick-assay` and `derrick-flow/code_review` consume.

```rust
#[async_trait]
impl Model for HostDelegatedModel {
    fn name(&self) -> &str { &self.name }
    fn provider(&self) -> &str { self.host }       // host name doubles as provider id
    fn cost_hint(&self) -> Option<&CostHint> { self.cost_hint.as_ref() }
    fn host_delegated_auth(&self) -> bool { true }

    async fn stream(&self, request: CompletionRequest) -> Result<CompletionStream, ModelError> {
        let host_req = self.build_host_request(&request);          // §4
        let host = self.host;

        // Run the adapter to completion, then map the single HostResponse into a
        // two-event stream. Do the await up front so errors surface from `stream()`
        // (matching the existing providers, where a spawn failure errors eagerly).
        let response: HostResponse = self
            .adapter
            .run(host_req)
            .await
            .map_err(|e| map_host_error(host, e))?;             // §7

        let text = response.stdout;                              // host stdout is the completion
        let end = CompletionEvent::End {
            tokens_in: response.tokens_in,
            tokens_out: response.tokens_out,
            finish_reason: FinishReason::Stop,
        };
        let events = vec![
            Ok(CompletionEvent::Content { text }),
            Ok(end),
        ];
        Ok(Box::pin(futures::stream::iter(events)))
    }
}
```

Key decisions:
- **Await `run()` inside `stream()`** rather than wrapping the future in the stream. `HostAdapter::run` is a single batch call returning one `HostResponse`; there is no incremental output to forward through the stream (the per-line `OutputSink` is a separate, host-layer concern and is not wired here). Awaiting up front means a `HostError::NotFound`/spawn failure is returned as the `Result` from `stream()`, exactly like the old `subprocess.rs` returned spawn errors eagerly. `complete()` then drains the trivially-ready `stream::iter`.
- **`stdout` is the response text.** The host CLIs print the model answer to stdout; `HostResponse.stderr` is diagnostic. On `NonZeroExit` the adapter already returns `Err`, so a non-zero exit never reaches the `Content` path.
- **`finish_reason` is always `Stop`** on success — the host gives no length signal. Keep `FinishReason::Length` reachable only if a future host reports truncation; not needed now.

### `provider()` returns `&str` — lifetime note

`Model::provider(&self) -> &str` borrows from `self`. Storing `host: &'static str` makes `fn provider(&self) -> &str { self.host }` trivially valid (`&'static str` coerces to the elided `&'_ str`). Do **not** store the host as an owned `String` unless you want a self-borrow — `&'static str` from the catalogue/registration is cleaner.

---

## 4. `HostRequest` construction from `CompletionRequest` + `ModelDef`

Host CLIs take **one** prompt argv item, so the system message, cached prefix, and prompt must be concatenated into `HostRequest.prompt`. Follow the existing `opencode.rs` ordering (system, then prefix, then prompt), each separated by a blank line, skipping empty segments:

```rust
impl HostDelegatedModel {
    fn build_host_request(&self, req: &CompletionRequest) -> HostRequest {
        let mut prompt = String::new();
        if let Some(system) = req.system.as_deref().filter(|s| !s.is_empty()) {
            prompt.push_str(system);
            prompt.push_str("\n\n");
        }
        if let Some(prefix) = req.cached_prefix.as_deref().filter(|s| !s.is_empty()) {
            prompt.push_str(prefix);
            prompt.push_str("\n\n");
        }
        prompt.push_str(&req.prompt);

        HostRequest {
            prompt,
            cwd: std::env::current_dir().unwrap_or_else(|_| std::path::PathBuf::from(".")),
            timeout: req.timeout,
            env: self.env.clone(),
            copilot_tools: CopilotToolPermission::AllowAll, // headless completions need non-interactive tools
            model: Some(self.name.clone()),                 // adapter applies catalogue.normalize()
            headless: true,                                  // completions are never interactive
            output_sink: None,
        }
    }
}
```

Field mapping:

| `HostRequest` field | source | note |
|---|---|---|
| `prompt` | `system` + `cached_prefix` + `prompt` | concatenated, blank-line separated, empties skipped |
| `cwd` | process cwd | completions are not repo-scoped; use cwd. (If a caller later needs a worktree dir, add a field to `CompletionRequest` — out of scope now.) |
| `timeout` | `request.timeout` | direct |
| `env` | `auth.env_map()` snapshot | forwards `GH_TOKEN`/proxy vars to the child (§5) |
| `copilot_tools` | `AllowAll` | required for headless copilot; ignored by other hosts |
| `model` | `Some(self.name)` | **un-normalised**; the adapter calls `catalogue.normalize(self.name(), model)` just before `--model` (per plan step 1). Provider passes the raw id. |
| `headless` | `true` | completion path is always non-interactive |
| `output_sink` | `None` | streaming not used on the model path |

**Important:** the provider must **not** normalise the model id itself — normalisation lives in the adapter (`claude.rs`/`codex.rs`/`copilot.rs`/`opencode.rs`/`aider.rs`). Sending the raw `self.name` keeps a single normalisation point and means the `derrick run` host path and the `Model` path behave identically. The plan's "5-host registry" test asserts `--model <normalized>` is passed; both call paths flow through the same adapter code.

---

## 5. `AuthStore` shrink

Drop `require`, `missing_required`, the `required: Vec<(String,String)>` field, and `ModelError::MissingCredential`. Keep `from_env`, `get`, `for_testing`, plus add one accessor the provider needs (`env_map`) so it can populate `HostRequest.env`.

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]
pub struct AuthStore {
    env: HashMap<String, Secret>,
    overrides: HashMap<(String, String), Secret>,
}

impl AuthStore {
    pub fn from_env() -> Self { /* unchanged, drop `required: Vec::new()` */ }
    pub fn for_testing(map: HashMap<(String, String), Secret>) -> Self { /* drop `required` */ }
    pub fn get(&self, provider: &str, key: &str) -> Option<&Secret> { /* unchanged */ }

    /// Plain `String` view of the env credentials, for forwarding to a host child process.
    pub fn env_map(&self) -> HashMap<String, String> {
        self.env.iter().map(|(k, v)| (k.clone(), v.expose().to_owned())).collect()
    }
}
```

**Call sites that still compile:** `derrick-assay/src/lib.rs:392`, `derrick-flow/src/code_review.rs:50`, `derrick-flow/src/steps.rs:296`, `derrick-adopt/src/lib.rs:181` all call only `AuthStore::from_env()` — unaffected. `derrick-cli/src/commands/doctor.rs:93-101` calls `require`/`missing_required` and **must be rewritten** (plan step 4 / step 6: replace the auth loop with the shared models-check core). `derrick-models/tests/models.rs` references `require`/`missing_required`/`MissingCredential` (lines 155-157, 206, 618-620) and must be updated/deleted alongside.

`ModelError::is_retryable` drops its `MissingCredential` arm — the remaining non-retryable set becomes `UnknownModel | UnknownRole | UnknownProvider | InvalidConfig`.

---

## 6. `resolve_role` / `ProviderRegistry::with_defaults`

The five host constructors are all `XHost::new() -> XHost` (unit structs). Register one closure per host name; each closure captures the host name and builds its adapter as an `Arc`. `shell` stays as-is.

```rust
impl ProviderRegistry {
    pub fn with_defaults() -> Self {
        let mut registry = Self::default();
        registry.register("shell", providers::shell::build);
        register_host(&mut registry, "claude",   || Arc::new(ClaudeHost::new()));
        register_host(&mut registry, "codex",    || Arc::new(CodexHost::new()));
        register_host(&mut registry, "copilot",  || Arc::new(CopilotHost::new()));
        register_host(&mut registry, "opencode", || Arc::new(OpencodeHost::new()));
        register_host(&mut registry, "aider",    || Arc::new(AiderHost::new()));
        registry
    }
}

/// Registers a host-delegated constructor under `host`. `make_adapter` builds a
/// fresh adapter each time a model is constructed (hosts are zero-cost units).
fn register_host(
    registry: &mut ProviderRegistry,
    host: &'static str,
    make_adapter: fn() -> Arc<dyn HostAdapter>,
) {
    registry.register(host, move |model_def: &ModelDef, auth: &AuthStore| {
        providers::host_delegated::build_for_host(host, make_adapter(), model_def, auth)
    });
}
```

This satisfies the existing `register<F>` bound: `F: Fn(&ModelDef, &AuthStore) -> Result<Box<dyn Model>, ModelError> + Send + Sync + 'static`. The closure captures `host: &'static str` (Copy, Send+Sync) and `make_adapter: fn()` (a function pointer, Send+Sync) — both satisfy the bound. `ProviderConstructor` (the stored `Arc<dyn Fn...>`) is unchanged.

`resolve_role` is **unchanged** — it already calls `ProviderRegistry::with_defaults().build(model_def, auth)`. The only behavioural change is which provider names resolve.

**`provider()` value must equal the host name.** Config's `ModelDef.provider()` now returns `"claude"`/`"codex"`/`"copilot"`/`"opencode"`/`"aider"` (per the `Config::defaults()` rewrite in plan step 3), and the registry keys match. The provider's `provider()`/`name()` accessors and the registry key are the same string, which is also the catalogue key and `HostAdapter::name()` — one identity across all four uses.

